for thee. And the Hereafter is for the righteousness.” (XX: 132).\"", "rawOcrText": "Part I (a)—lmportance of Salaat 19 \"And enjoin salaat upon thy people and be thyself constant therein. We ask not of to provide suste- nance. We provide it for thee. And the Hereafter •is for (xx:.132). the righteousness.\" It is said in a hadith that when somebody is confronted with a need, whether pertaining to this life or the Hereafter, or whether it concerns Allah or a mortal, he should per- form a perfect wudhu, offer salaat of two rakaats, glorify Allah, then ask blessing for the Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam), and then pray as under: \"There is no god save Allah—the Clemént—the Bounti- ful. Glorified be Allah, the Lord of the tremendous throne. Praise be to Allah, the Lord of the worlds. I ask Thee all that leadeth to Thy Mercy and deserveth Thy forgiveness. I ask Thee abundance in all that is good and refuge from all that is evil. Leave me no sin but Thou pardOnest it, and no distress but Thou removest it, and no need but Thou fulfillest it. O, most Merciful of those who show mercy!\" Wahb bin Munabbih writes: \"Have your needs fulfilled by Allah through salaat. In the good old time, if a calamity befell the people, they would hurry towards salaat.\" It said that in Koofah there was a porter who was well known for his honesty. People trusted him with their valuables and money, which he carried from one place to another. Once he was on his usual errand when a person met him on the way and asked him about his destination. When the porter gave him the required information, he said, \"I am also bound for the same destination. If I could walk, I would have accompanied you on foot. Will you kindly give me a lift on your mule for one dinaar?\" The porter agreed and allowed him to share the mule with him. They came to a crossing on the way. The person said, ''Now, which road will you take?'' ''The main road, of course,'' replied the porter. The person said, ''No, brother. We should go by the", "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0190000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 1, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "It is said in a hadith that when somebody is confronted with a need, whether pertaining to this life or the Hereafter, or whether it concerns Allah or a mortal, he should perform a perfect wudhu, offer salaat of two rakaats, glorify Allah, then ask blessing for the Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam), and then pray as under:", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0190000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 2, "blockType": "arabic", "languageCode": "ar", "direction": "rtl", "textContent": "لَا إِلٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ الْحَلِيمُ الْكَرِيمُ سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَرْشِ الْعَظِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ أَسْأَلُكَ مُوجِبَاتِ رَحْمَتِكَ وَعَزَائِمَ مَغْفِرَتِكَ وَالْغَنِيمَةَ مِنْ كُلِّ بِرٍّ وَالسَّلَامَةَ مِنْ كُلِّ إِثْمٍ لَا تَدَعْ لِي ذَنْبًا إِلَّا غَفَرْتَهُ وَلَا هَمًّا إِلَّا فَرَّجْتَهُ وَلَا حَاجَةً هِيَ لَكَ رِضًا إِلَّا قَضَيْتَهَا يَا أَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِينَ", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0190000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 3, "blockType": "translation", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "\"There is no god save Allah—the Clement—the Bountiful. Glorified be Allah, the Lord of the tremendous throne. Praise be to Allah, the Lord of the worlds. I ask Thee all that leadeth to Thy Mercy and deserveth Thy forgiveness. I ask Thee abundance in all that is good and refuge from all that is evil. Leave me no sin but Thou pardonest it, and no distress but Thou removest it, and no need but Thou fulfillest it. O, most Merciful of those who show mercy!\"", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0190000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 4, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "Wahb bin Munabbih writes: \"Have your needs fulfilled by Allah through salaat. In the good old time, if a calamity befell the people, they would hurry towards salaat.\" It is said that in Koofah there was a porter who was well known for his honesty. People trusted him with their valuables and money, which he carried from one place to another. Once he was on his usual errand when a person met him on the way and asked him about his destination. When the porter gave him the required information, he said, \"I am also bound for the same destination. If I could walk, I would have accompanied you on foot. Will you kindly give me a lift on your mule for one dinaar?\" The porter agreed and allowed him to share the mule with him. They came to a crossing on the way. The person said, ''Now, which road will you take?'' ''The main road, of course,'' replied the porter. The person said, ''No, brother. We should go by the", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}], "pageNumber": 19}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO temp_import_pages VALUES ('{"pageId": "c0200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "bookPartId": "59d7f112-fb58-4726-9c35-11485c6155e7", "sourceShowsPrintedPageLabel": true, "printedPageLabel": "20", "printedPageNumber": 20, "pdfPageNumber": 403, "sequenceIndex": 10, "verificationStatus": "in_review", "scanLanguages": ["en", "ar"], "reviewEvidence": {"englishTranscriptionComplete": true, "arabicTranscriptionComplete": true, "blockOrderChecked": true, "identifiersChecked": true, "scanCompared": true, "provenanceNotes": "Canonical transcription checked against the rendered secondary scan; master PDF page mapping retained. Fine vocalisation is preserved where clearly legible; no unresolved Arabic word or letter remains. English OCR correction and Arabic/translation-pair classification pass compared against the rendered source scan; raw OCR retained unchanged."}, "structuralNodes": [{"id": "a6000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "parentId": null, "nodeType": "section", "sourceLabel": "PART I", "sourceIdentifier": "I", "title": "IMPORTANCE OF SALAAT", "sequenceIndex": 0, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}, {"id": "a6100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "parentId": "a6000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "nodeType": "chapter", "sourceLabel": "CHAPTER I", "sourceIdentifier": "I", "title": "THE REWARDS OF SALAAT", "sequenceIndex": 1, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}, {"id": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "parentId": "a6100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "nodeType": "hadith", "sourceLabel": "Hadith.—V", "sourceIdentifier": "V", "title": null, "sequenceIndex": 2, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}], "blocks": [{"id": "d0200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 0, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "other road which is a shortcut and there is plenty of grass. enroute to feed the animal.'' The porter said, ''I have never been on this path.'' The person remarked, ''But I have travelled by this route quite often''. The porter believed him and put the animal on that path. After some distance, the path ended in a terrifying forest where a large number of dead bodies were lying about. All of a sudden the person jumped down from the mule and took out his knife with the intention of slaying the porter. ''Hold your hand'', shouted the porter, ''Take the animal and its load, but do not kill me''. The person refused to listen to his entreaty and swore that he would first kill the porter and then take possession of the animal and the goods. Seeing that his entreaties fell on deaf ears and that his cruel heart would not melt, the porter said to him, ''All right if you must kill me, then permit to say my salaat of only two rakaats.'' The person agreed and remarked, ''You can please yourself. All the dead you see over here made the same request, but their salaat was of no avail to them.''. The porter started the salaat, but could not recollect any soorah to connect with the Fatihah, in spite-of his best efforts. Meanwhile the person grew impatient and pressed him hard to hurry up with the salaat. All of a sudden the following verse flashed to his mind:", "rawOcrText": "20 Virtues of Salaat other road which is a shortcut and there is plenty of grass. enroute to feed the animal.'' The porter said, ''I hive never been on this path.'' The person remarked, ''But I have travel- led by this route quite often''. The porter believed him and put the animal on that path. After some distance, the path ended in a terrifying forest where a large number of dead bodies were lying about. All of a sudden the person jumped down from the mule and took out his knife with the intention of slaying the porter. ''Hold your hand'', shouted the porter, ''Take the animal and its load, but do not kill me''. The person refused to listen to his entreaty and swore that he would first kill the porter and then take possession of the animal and the goods. Seeing that his en- treaties fell on deaf ears and that his cruel heart would not melt, the porter said to him, ''All right if you must kill me, then permit to say my salaat of only two rakaats.'' The person ageed and remarked, ''You can please yourself. All the dead you see over here made the same request, but their salaat was of no avail to them.''. The porter started the salaat, but could not recollect any soorah to connect with the Fatihah, in spite-of his best efforts. Meanwhile the person grew impatient and pressed him hard to hurry up with the salaat. All of a sudden the following verse flashed to his mind: ''Is it not He Who answereth the vwonged one when he crieth unto Him, and removeth the evil. •.. (XXVII-62). The porter was reciting the verse and the tears welled up in his eyes, when a horseman suddenly appeared on the scene. He was wearing a glittering helmet and held a spear in his hand. He pierced the body of the pitiless rogue with his spear and killed him there and then. A flame of fire rose from the spot where•the dead body fell. The porter fell down prostrate and thanked Allah. After finishing his salaat, he ran towards the horseman and requested him to disclose his identity. He replied, ''I am a slave to Him •who answereth the wronged one. You are now safe and can go wherever you like.'' Saying this, the horseman rode away and disappeared.\" Indeed salaat is a tremendous asset. Besides pleasing Allah it often gets us deliverence from the calamities of this", "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 1, "blockType": "arabic", "languageCode": "ar", "direction": "rtl", "textContent": "أَمَّنْ يُجِيبُ الْمُضْطَرَّ إِذَا دَعَاهُ وَيَكْشِفُ السُّوءَ", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 2, "blockType": "translation", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "''Is it not He Who answereth the wronged one when he crieth unto Him, and removeth the evil . . .” (XXVII-62).", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006", "sequenceIndex": 3, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "The porter was reciting the verse and the tears welled up in his eyes, when a horseman suddenly appeared on the scene. He was wearing a glittering helmet and held a spear in his hand. He pierced the body of the pitiless rogue with his spear and killed him there and then. A flame of fire rose from the spot where the dead body fell. The porter fell down prostrate and thanked Allah. After finishing his salaat, he ran towards the horseman and requested him to disclose his identity. He replied, ''I am a slave to Him who answereth the wronged one. You are now safe and can go wherever you like.'' Saying this, the horseman rode away and disappeared.\" Indeed salaat is a tremendous asset. Besides pleasing Allah it often gets us deliverence from the calamities of this", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}], "pageNumber": 20}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO temp_import_pages VALUES ('{"pageId": "c0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "bookPartId": "59d7f112-fb58-4726-9c35-11485c6155e7", "sourceShowsPrintedPageLabel": true, "printedPageLabel": "21", "printedPageNumber": 21, "pdfPageNumber": 404, "sequenceIndex": 11, "verificationStatus": "in_review", "scanLanguages": ["en", "ar"], "reviewEvidence": {"englishTranscriptionComplete": true, "arabicTranscriptionComplete": true, "blockOrderChecked": true, "identifiersChecked": true, "scanCompared": true, "provenanceNotes": "Canonical transcription checked against the rendered secondary scan; master PDF page mapping retained. Fine vocalisation is preserved where clearly legible; no unresolved Arabic word or letter remains."}, "structuralNodes": [{"id": "a6000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "parentId": null, "nodeType": "section", "sourceLabel": "PART I", "sourceIdentifier": "I", "title": "IMPORTANCE OF SALAAT", "sequenceIndex": 0, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}, {"id": "a6100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "parentId": "a6000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "nodeType": "chapter", "sourceLabel": "CHAPTER I", "sourceIdentifier": "I", "title": "THE REWARDS OF SALAAT", "sequenceIndex": 1, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}, {"id": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "parentId": "a6100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "nodeType": "hadith", "sourceLabel": "Hadith.—VI", "sourceIdentifier": "VI", "title": null, "sequenceIndex": 2, "startPageId": null, "endPageId": null}], "blocks": [{"id": "d0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "sequenceIndex": 0, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "life and provides us with tranquility and peace of mind. Ibn Seereen writes: \"If I be allowed to choose between Paradise and salaat of two rakaats, I would prefer salaat. The reason is quite clear. Paradise is for my own pleasure while salaat is for the pleasure of my dear Lord.\" The Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) has said: \"Enviable is the lot of that Muslim who is with least encumbrance, whose main fortune is salaat, who remains content with humble provision throughout his life, who worships his Lord in a dutiful manner, who lives a nameless life and who dies an early death, with very little to bequeath and very few to mourn him.\" In another hadith, the Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) is reported to have said: ''Offer your salaat at your homes quite frequently, so that these may be blessed with Allah''s Grace and Mercy. \"", "rawOcrText": "Part I (a)—lmportance of Salaat 21 life and provides us with tranquility and peace of mind. Ibn Seereen writes: \"If I be allowed to choose between Paradise and salaat of two rakaats, I would prefer salaat. The reason is quite clear. Paradise is for my own pleasure while salaat is for the pleasure of my dear Lord.\" The Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) has said: \"Enviable is the lot of that Muslim who is with least encumbrance, whose main fortune is salaat, who remains content with humble provision throughout his life, who worships his Lord in a dutiful manner, who lives a nameless life and who dies an early death, with very little to bequeath and very few to mourn him.\" In anothef hadith, the Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) is reported to have said: ''Offer your salaat at your homes quite frequently, so that these may be blessed with Allah''s Grace and Mercy. \" Hadith.—VI \"Abu Muslim narrates: I went to see Abu Umaamah (Radhiyallaho anho) while he was in the musjid. I asked him if he had really heard the Holy Prophet (Sal- lallaho alaihe wasallam) saying, \"When a person per- forms wudhu with right performance and then says his fardh salaat, Allah forgives him all the sins committed that day by his feet in going towards evil, by his hands in doing evil, by his ears in listening to evil, by his eyes in looking at evil and by his heart in thinking of evil.'' He replied, ''By Allah, I have heard these words from the Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) again and again. \" Many of the companions have narrated this Hadith with slight variations. Those endowed with the power of Kashf can even witness the sins being shed. It is said of", "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "sequenceIndex": 1, "blockType": "heading", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "Hadith.—VI", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "sequenceIndex": 2, "blockType": "arabic", "languageCode": "ar", "direction": "rtl", "textContent": "عَنْ أَبِي مُسْلِمٍ الْخَوْلَانِيِّ رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ قَالَ دَخَلْتُ عَلَى أَبِي أُمَامَةَ وَهُوَ فِي الْمَسْجِدِ فَقُلْتُ يَا أَبَا أُمَامَةَ إِنَّ رَجُلًا حَدَّثَنِي عَنْكَ أَنَّكَ سَمِعْتَ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ ﷺ يَقُولُ مَنْ تَوَضَّأَ فَأَسْبَغَ الْوُضُوءَ فَغَسَلَ يَدَيْهِ وَوَجْهَهُ وَمَسَحَ عَلَى رَأْسِهِ وَأُذُنَيْهِ ثُمَّ قَامَ إِلَى صَلَاةٍ مَفْرُوضَةٍ غَفَرَ اللَّهُ لَهُ فِي ذَلِكَ الْيَوْمِ مَا مَشَتْ إِلَيْهِ رِجْلَاهُ وَقَبَضَتْ عَلَيْهِ يَدَاهُ وَسَمِعَتْ إِلَيْهِ أُذُنَاهُ وَنَظَرَتْ إِلَيْهِ عَيْنَاهُ وَحَدَّثَتْ بِهِ نَفْسُهُ مِنْ سُوءٍ فَقَالَ وَاللَّهِ لَقَدْ سَمِعْتُهُ مِنَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مِرَارًا", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "sequenceIndex": 3, "blockType": "arabic", "languageCode": "ar", "direction": "rtl", "textContent": "رواه أحمد والطبراني على سنده الحسن", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}, {"id": "d0210000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005", "structuralNodeId": "a6200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007", "sequenceIndex": 4, "blockType": "english", "languageCode": "en", "direction": "ltr", "textContent": "\"Abu Muslim narrates: I went to see Abu Umaamah (Radhiyallaho anho) while he was in the musjid. I asked him if he had really heard the Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) saying, \"When a person performs wudhu with right performance and then says his fardh salaat, Allah forgives him all the sins committed that day by his feet in going towards evil, by his hands in doing evil, by his ears in listening to evil, by his eyes in looking at evil and by his heart in thinking of evil.'' He replied, ''By Allah, I have heard these words from the Holy Prophet (Sallallaho alaihe wasallam) again and again. \" Many of the companions have narrated this Hadith with slight variations. Those endowed with the power of Kashf can even witness the sins being shed. It is said of", "rawOcrText": null, "ttsEligible": true, "verificationStatus": "in_review"}], "pageNumber": 21}'::jsonb);
